WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:01.670
It looks like I'm in the middle of nowhere,

00:00:01.670 --> 00:00:03.050
and I guess I am.

00:00:03.050 --> 00:00:05.150
About a mile that way is Qaanaaq,

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:06.950
on the west coast of Greenland,

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:09.590
about 700 miles north of the Arctic Circle.

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:12.920
The town has more sled dogs than people; the
locals, some of them,

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:16.680
still hunt seals and narwhal when the ice
retreats in the summer.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:20.589
And just outside the town, someone is listening
for nuclear explosions.

00:00:20.589 --> 00:00:26.359
In 1989, 183 countries signed the Comprehensive
Nuclear Test Ban Treaty.

00:00:26.359 --> 00:00:29.390
The Cold War had just ended, and with the
threat of nuclear apocalypse

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:30.829
seeming just a little less likely,

00:00:30.829 --> 00:00:34.970
the idea of the treaty was to completely ban
nuclear explosions,

00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:37.020
even for research.

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:38.020
Two decades later,

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:40.270
and the treaty is still not enforced, because
politics.

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:43.070
But the people who drafted the treaty thought
that might happen,

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:45.590
and created the Preparatory Commission,

00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:48.270
whose job it is to get everything in place
for when the treaty

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:50.870
hopefully, eventually, comes into force --

00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:52.920
and even if it never actually happens,

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:55.570
to try and do some good anyway.

00:00:55.570 --> 00:00:58.180
And this over here is one of the results:

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:01.160
IS18, an infrasound listening station,

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:03.970
part of a network that spans the planet.

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:07.829
It’s a microbarograph: a set of very precise
air pressure sensors,

00:01:07.829 --> 00:01:10.280
and it listens for very low frequency sound
waves,

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:12.030
far below the range of human hearing.

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:13.030
They’re less like sound,

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:15.759
and more like massive disturbances in the
air

00:01:15.759 --> 00:01:18.869
that travel round the globe after enormous
explosions.

00:01:18.869 --> 00:01:21.240
I'm an electronics engineer,

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:28.289
and my job here, now, is to maintain the infrasound
station.

00:01:28.289 --> 00:01:33.479
If you just put a microbarometer in the air,

00:01:33.479 --> 00:01:37.790
the noise from the wind will be killing every
signal.

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:42.740
So we have these arrays, we have 96 inlets.

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:48.009
It all goes to the barometer.

00:01:48.009 --> 00:01:54.149
Because we have that many inlets, it is not
really probable

00:01:54.149 --> 00:02:01.639
that the wind noise will be exactly the same
at all those inlets

00:02:01.639 --> 00:02:04.499
so they are fed to a central manifold

00:02:04.499 --> 00:02:08.960
and the wind will sort-of cancel out.

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:11.030
It really does!

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:13.140
Detecting something doesn’t mean that there’s
been a nuclear test —

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:15.970
it might be a meteor breaking up, a volcano
erupting,

00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:19.030
or even a massive iceberg calving off a glacier
nearby —

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:21.600
but if it can't be explained like that,

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:24.710
then governments should start asking questions.

00:02:24.710 --> 00:02:27.600
Results from here go to the Commission’s
headquarters in Vienna,

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:29.200
via a base in the middle of town,

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:30.760
where they’re combined with those

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:33.770
from the dozens of other listening stations
around the planet.

00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:36.830
And as well as that, the commission uses hydroacoustic
sensors

00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:38.760
to detect underwater explosions,

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:42.010
seismic stations that detect earthquakes from
nuclear tests,

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:43.760
and radionuclide detection stations

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:46.590
that check the level of radioactivity in the
air.

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:49.480
You can see from the time difference

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:56.090
when the individual stations detect the pressure
wave.

00:02:56.090 --> 00:03:00.700
From that you can deduct what direction it's
coming form.

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:03.790
The nuclear test ban treaty, if it ever actually
comes into force,

00:03:03.790 --> 00:03:05.750
will apply around the world.

00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:07.210
Even up here in the Arctic.

00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:10.410
And theoretically, some rogue government could
be ridiculous enough

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:13.340
to try and get away with a nuclear test in
the Arctic.

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:14.800
After all, back in the 20th century,

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:17.850
about a fifth of all Soviet nuclear tests
were in the Russian Arctic –

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:22.170
and that included Tsar Bomba, the largest
nuclear bomb in history.

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:24.600
Even if new testing in the Arctic is unlikely,

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:27.950
data from here can still help triangulate
other explosions.

00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:31.960
When you’re designing a network to keep
watch on the planet,

00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:33.820
not just where most of the people live,

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:36.200
that network really does have to be global.

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:38.840
Even if that means setting up a research base

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:42.610
in one of the most remote locations on Earth.

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:45.330
I'm here because of Chris Hadfield's Generator
Arctic!

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:48.150
It's an amazing chance to be here on the top
of the world,

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:50.010
Ellesmere Island, glacier right behind us,

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:52.530
and we're going to put on a show in Toronto.

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:53.530
Massey Hall, November 12th.

00:03:53.530 --> 00:03:55.680
And apart from that, go see all the videos

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:57.540
from all the people who were on this expedition.

00:03:57.540 --> 00:03:59.760
Links are on screen and in the description
now.

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:00.760
Thank you so much!

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:01.760
My pleasure.

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:02.260
Nice to be here!

